OK, let's talk about regional differences.

730
00:38:01,350 --> 00:38:05,720
I thought this was fascinating how all right changes completely based on how you say it.

731
00:38:05,920 --> 00:38:08,840
Yeah, this is a brilliant example of tone being everything.

732
00:38:09,080 --> 00:38:13,310
All right is huge in Britain, obviously, but you hear it more widely now.

733
00:38:13,610 --> 00:38:14,490
So how does it work?

734
00:38:15,170 --> 00:38:21,820
If you say it with your voice going up at the end, all right, it's just a friendly, general hello.

735
00:38:22,000 --> 00:38:23,740
Like, how are you, basically.

736
00:38:23,780 --> 00:38:30,710
But if you use softer tone, maybe a bit more level or concerned, all right, then it's asking if they're okay.

737
00:38:31,050 --> 00:38:32,880
Similar to you good.

738
00:38:33,440 --> 00:38:33,700
Wow.

739
00:38:33,940 --> 00:38:36,000
So the same two words do double duty.

740
00:38:36,220 --> 00:38:36,820
Completely.

741
00:38:36,860 --> 00:38:38,990
It's all in the vocal music, setting the intention.

742
00:38:39,150 --> 00:38:40,110
Very versatile.

743
00:38:40,260 --> 00:38:44,850
And the last one in this greeting section for a deeper check in, maybe if you haven't seen someone for a bit.

744
00:38:45,290 --> 00:38:46,170
How have you been keeping?

745
00:38:46,990 --> 00:38:49,990
Ah, adding that keeping just elevates it, doesn't it?

746
00:38:50,090 --> 00:38:51,000
Yeah, it sounds gentler.

747
00:38:51,200 --> 00:38:51,560
It does.

748
00:38:51,620 --> 00:38:53,160
Extra gentle, more sincere.

749
00:38:53,200 --> 00:38:58,030
It shows you're interested in their well-being over time, not just in this exact second.

750
00:38:58,050 --> 00:39:00,410
And the source stresses it's not being nosy.

751
00:39:00,580 --> 00:39:01,020
Right.

752
00:39:01,120 --> 00:39:03,950
It explicitly says it's not a nosy question.

753
00:39:04,070 --> 00:39:06,480
It's just a warm, thoughtful invitation.

754
00:39:06,580 --> 00:39:12,260
Like, tell me how things have been since we last properly talked, but without any pressure to overshare.

755
00:39:12,390 --> 00:39:12,930
OK, wow.

756
00:39:13,090 --> 00:39:19,840
So that's 10 different ways just to say hello or check in from super casual to really quite sincere.

757
00:39:20,220 --> 00:39:21,060
Now we've opened the door.

758
00:39:21,220 --> 00:39:22,220
Yeah.

759
00:39:22,240 --> 00:39:23,320
We need something to talk about.

760
00:39:23,620 --> 00:39:23,760
Yeah.

761
00:39:23,800 --> 00:39:27,170
So part two, building the bridge, fueling small talk.

762
00:39:27,310 --> 00:39:27,530
Yes.

763
00:39:27,910 --> 00:39:28,590
Small talk.

764
00:39:28,690 --> 00:39:30,490
It gets a bad rap sometimes, doesn't it?

765
00:39:30,700 --> 00:39:34,900
But in the context of this source material, it's really important to understand its purpose.

766
00:39:34,940 --> 00:39:36,520
Which isn't deep philosophical debate.

767
00:39:36,500 --> 00:39:37,220
Definitely not.

768
00:39:37,300 --> 00:39:43,670
It's that gentle conversation about simple, neutral things, the weather, maybe your surroundings.

769
00:39:43,950 --> 00:39:45,230
Its main job is relational.

770
00:39:45,330 --> 00:39:45,890
Relational.

771
00:39:45,910 --> 00:39:53,520
Yeah, building that little bridge, as the source puts it, between people, making everyone feel comfortable, connected, smoothing the social gears.

772
00:39:53,880 --> 00:39:58,500
And the undisputed world champion topic for Small Talk has to be the weather.

773
00:39:58,880 --> 00:39:59,740
Always the weather.

774
00:40:00,140 --> 00:40:01,630
Starting with the absolute classic.

775
00:40:02,010 --> 00:40:03,070
Nice weather today, isn't it?

776
00:40:03,270 --> 00:40:04,490
It's a classic for a reason.

777
00:40:04,590 --> 00:40:05,950
It's totally universal.

778
00:40:06,310 --> 00:40:07,070
It's positive.

779
00:40:07,090 --> 00:40:07,680
It's easy.

780
00:40:07,820 --> 00:40:08,960
Anyone can agree with it.

781
00:40:09,300 --> 00:40:12,100
Your boss, a stranger at the bus stop.

782
00:40:12,120 --> 00:40:12,640
Doesn't matter.

783
00:40:13,130 --> 00:40:16,710
It's the lowest possible risk way to just make a tiny connection.

784
00:40:17,390 --> 00:40:19,840
You're agreeing on a shared, pleasant reality.

785
00:40:20,020 --> 00:40:21,140
Great starting point.

786
00:40:21,290 --> 00:40:24,230
But, okay, sometimes the weather is genuinely awful.

787
00:40:24,840 --> 00:40:29,020
And the source gives us a really strong word for that shared complaint.

788
00:40:29,240 --> 00:40:30,140
It's brutal out there.

789
00:40:30,480 --> 00:40:36,230
Using brutal for really bad heat or cold or wind, it's quite clever socially.

790
00:40:36,250 --> 00:40:37,010
Why is that?

791
00:40:37,030 --> 00:40:42,620
Because it immediately creates this sense of shared experience, an instant bond, the source calls it.

792
00:40:42,900 --> 00:40:44,280
You're not just describing the weather.

793
00:40:44,320 --> 00:40:47,590
You're using that strong, slightly dramatic word.

794
00:40:47,700 --> 00:40:48,870
Yeah, brutal is strong.

795
00:40:48,850 --> 00:40:49,770
To show solidarity.

796
00:40:49,790 --> 00:40:52,950
You're basically saying, wow, we're both going through this tough thing together, aren't we?

797
00:40:53,290 --> 00:40:57,120
And sharing that difficulty, even a minor one, builds comfort really quickly.

798
00:40:57,180 --> 00:40:57,840
It's empathetic.

799
00:40:57,960 --> 00:41:02,440
And to maybe lighten that mood a bit, we've got the playful icebreaker, hot enough for you.

800
00:41:03,580 --> 00:41:04,060
Ah, yes.

801
00:41:04,530 --> 00:41:06,490
This is definitely a lighthearted joke.

802
00:41:06,510 --> 00:41:08,490
And the way it works socially is key.

803
00:41:08,510 --> 00:41:11,950
The future relies on the fact that it's obviously hot enough for everyone.

804
00:41:11,990 --> 00:41:15,200
It's stating the obvious in a questioning way.

805
00:41:15,300 --> 00:41:18,280
It's like a little shared nod to the ridiculousness of the heat.

806
00:41:18,480 --> 00:41:20,390
But delivery is crucial.

807
00:41:20,630 --> 00:41:21,550
Absolutely crucial.

808
00:41:21,670 --> 00:41:22,810
The source stresses.

809
00:41:22,870 --> 00:41:25,360
You must say it with a smile.

810
00:41:26,100 --> 00:41:27,950
Maybe a little eye roll or a chuckle.

811
00:41:29,040 --> 00:41:29,780
Otherwise.

812
00:41:30,030 --> 00:41:33,470
It just sounds like a really weird, maybe slightly aggressive question.

813
00:41:34,070 --> 00:41:35,860
Like, are you complaining about the heat?

814
00:41:36,080 --> 00:41:40,770
But said with that light touch, it's just acknowledging the shared discomfort without actually complaining.

815
00:41:40,910 --> 00:41:41,090
Got it.

816
00:41:41,730 --> 00:41:44,890
Similar vibe maybe for when you're fighting a gale force wind.

817
00:41:45,220 --> 00:41:46,440
Crazy wind, right?

818
00:41:46,540 --> 00:41:46,920
Yeah.

819
00:41:47,100 --> 00:41:49,360
And the key word there is right at the end.

820
00:41:49,340 --> 00:41:50,140
Why right?

821
00:41:50,160 --> 00:41:52,370
That little tag turns it into an invitation.

822
00:41:52,970 --> 00:41:53,070
Yeah.

823
00:41:53,090 --> 00:41:59,700
You're inviting the other person to agree, to say, yeah, it's nuts, to validate your experience of struggling against this crazy wind.

824
00:41:59,720 --> 00:42:03,630
You're looking for that little moment of this wind solidarity.

825
00:42:03,870 --> 00:42:07,580
It's interesting, though, not all bad weather needs complaining about.

826
00:42:07,600 --> 00:42:12,250
The source gives us ways to be optimistic, like we needed this rain.

827
00:42:12,380 --> 00:42:14,960
That's a really lovely positive reframe, isn't it?

828
00:42:15,700 --> 00:42:19,230
Instead of just rain again, it shows a bigger perspective.

829
00:42:19,270 --> 00:42:27,580
You're acknowledging that, OK, maybe it's inconvenient for us right now, but the environment, the plants, the gardens, they needed it.

830
00:42:27,760 --> 00:42:29,570
So it positions you as someone thoughtful.

831
00:42:30,030 --> 00:42:30,550
Exactly.

832
00:42:30,650 --> 00:42:34,550
Someone who sees the good, not just the personal hassle.

833
00:42:34,700 --> 00:42:41,580
And for a day that's maybe just gray and cloudy, not actually raining, there's that hopeful visual one.

834
00:42:42,120 --> 00:42:43,080
Sun's trying to come out.

835
00:42:43,220 --> 00:42:44,260
I love the imagery in that.

836
00:42:44,520 --> 00:42:45,240
Trying.

837
00:42:45,260 --> 00:42:51,050
It makes the sun sound like it's making an effort, like it's actively working hard to appear from behind the clouds.

838
00:42:51,730 --> 00:42:56,280
It's just a nice, quiet way to inject a little optimism into a potentially gloomy moment.

839
00:42:57,200 --> 00:42:58,150
Lifts the spirits.

840
00:42:58,380 --> 00:43:01,640
Small talk often shifts from just observing to implying activities.

841
00:43:02,060 --> 00:43:03,930
Good day for the beach seems like one of those.

842
00:43:03,970 --> 00:43:04,430
Definitely.

843
00:43:04,450 --> 00:43:06,890
That phrase instantly paints a picture, doesn't it?

844
00:43:07,150 --> 00:43:09,450
Sun, sand, sea, happy thoughts.

845
00:43:10,200 --> 00:43:18,190
And because it paints that picture, it naturally invites a slightly longer chat, maybe about hobbies, weekend plans, what you like doing on sunny days.

846
00:43:18,770 --> 00:43:22,410
It opens up a new path for connection beyond just, yeah, it's sunny.

847
00:43:22,390 --> 00:43:29,100
Okay, this section then cleverly links this weather chat directly into polite ways to end the conversation, depending on the weather.

848
00:43:29,740 --> 00:43:31,280
First, on a hot day.

849
00:43:32,240 --> 00:43:33,180
Stay cool today.

850
00:43:33,220 --> 00:43:36,350
Yeah, it's a perfect little add-on to your goodbye, a caring thought.

851
00:43:36,430 --> 00:43:38,170
You've maybe just been talking about how hot it is.

852
00:43:38,270 --> 00:43:38,590
Right.

853
00:43:39,110 --> 00:43:44,580
So saying stay cool acknowledges that difficulty again and expresses a simple hope for their comfort.

854
00:43:45,180 --> 00:43:46,180
Hope you don't overheat.

855
00:43:47,020 --> 00:43:50,440
It's a small, kind gesture you can use with pretty much anyone.

856
00:43:50,490 --> 00:43:55,950
And if the weather is actually dangerous, fog, snow, ice, we switch to drive safe out there.

857
00:43:56,300 --> 00:44:02,520
That shows immediate practical concern for their safety because driving in really bad weather is risky.

858
00:44:02,720 --> 00:44:03,060
Yeah.

859
00:44:03,080 --> 00:44:05,820
So saying this just communicates, hey, I care about you.

860
00:44:05,840 --> 00:44:07,690
I want you to get where you're going securely.

861
00:44:08,070 --> 00:44:14,510
It's a very kind, considered way to say goodbye, especially if someone's leaving the office or a meeting to head home in bad conditions.

862
00:44:14,530 --> 00:44:17,800
And the last one in this weather goodbye link, let's hope it holds.

863
00:44:17,880 --> 00:44:21,110
You use this when the weather is nice now, but maybe looks a bit iffy.

864
00:44:21,170 --> 00:44:23,310
You see some dark clouds rolling in, perhaps.

865
00:44:23,810 --> 00:44:31,300
If you're at an outdoor event or planning something outside, saying, let's hope it holds, creates this little shared positive wish.

866
00:44:31,550 --> 00:44:34,290
You're united in hoping the good weather lasts.

867
00:44:34,310 --> 00:44:34,590
Right.

868
00:44:34,610 --> 00:44:35,710
So we've made contact.

869
00:44:35,750 --> 00:44:37,640
We've built a little bridge with small talk.

870
00:44:38,020 --> 00:44:39,440
But now we hit the big one.

871
00:44:39,960 --> 00:44:43,260
The question that seems simple but trips so many people up.

872
00:44:43,600 --> 00:44:44,200
How are you?

873
00:44:44,990 --> 00:44:50,640
Part three is all about the real answers to how are you moving way beyond just I'm fine.

874
00:44:50,610 --> 00:44:51,180
Yes.

875
00:44:52,080 --> 00:44:54,120
This is where authenticity really comes into play.

876
00:44:54,420 --> 00:44:55,940
The source material is quite firm on this.

877
00:44:56,320 --> 00:45:00,270
I'm fine, thank you is, well, it's correct grammatically.

878
00:45:00,850 --> 00:45:03,980
But it often sounds like a closed door, a bit robotic maybe.

879
00:45:04,020 --> 00:45:08,300
Native speakers, it points out, have so many other colors in their answer box.

880
00:45:08,280 --> 00:45:08,840
I like that.

881
00:45:09,040 --> 00:45:17,960
Learning these different shades, these alternatives is how you start to sound really natural, how you express your true feelings appropriately, of course, with social awareness.

882
00:45:18,040 --> 00:45:22,310
OK, let's start with what the source calls the gold standard, the most common natural positive answer.

883
00:45:22,670 --> 00:45:22,970
Pretty good.

884
00:45:22,990 --> 00:45:23,250
Thanks.

885
00:45:23,810 --> 00:45:24,830
Yeah, pretty good.

886
00:45:25,130 --> 00:45:28,060
It's kind of the default positive response in natural conversation.

887
00:45:28,120 --> 00:45:32,700
Interestingly, pretty good in this context usually means very good.

888
00:45:32,950 --> 00:45:33,510
Oh, really?

889
00:45:33,530 --> 00:45:34,700
Not just OK?

890
00:45:34,980 --> 00:45:43,090
No, it's stronger than just OK, but it avoids sounding overly enthusiastic, like yelling fantastic, which can sometimes feel a bit much.

891
00:45:43,430 --> 00:45:43,610
Right.

892
00:45:43,630 --> 00:45:45,350
Pretty good is friendly.

893
00:45:45,410 --> 00:45:46,280
It's warm.

894
00:45:46,320 --> 00:45:47,160
It sounds natural.

895
00:45:47,280 --> 00:45:50,440
And adding the thanks shows you appreciate them asking.

896
00:45:50,920 --> 00:45:58,780
It's just the perfect, simple, upbeat answer for most everyday positive situations where you're doing well, but don't need to shout about it.

897
00:45:58,760 --> 00:45:59,220
Okay.

898
00:45:59,600 --> 00:46:07,110
Then for showing your content, but maybe in a more humble way, we have Can't Complain and the slightly funnier one, Not Too Shabby.

899
00:46:07,300 --> 00:46:08,140
Both great options.

900
00:46:08,380 --> 00:46:08,960
Can't complain.

901
00:46:09,460 --> 00:46:09,560
Yeah.

902
00:46:09,580 --> 00:46:11,640
Basically signals, yep, life's generally good.

903
00:46:11,900 --> 00:46:13,240
No major issues to report.

904
00:46:13,460 --> 00:46:16,190
It shows your content upbeat, but keeping it low key.

905
00:46:16,410 --> 00:46:17,330
And not too shabby.

906
00:46:17,530 --> 00:46:18,450
That one's got a bit of humor.

907
00:46:18,730 --> 00:46:21,320
Some shabby means sort of poor quality or rundown.

908
00:46:21,700 --> 00:46:27,580
Saying you're not too shabby is a classic bit of British understatement, meaning you're actually doing quite well.

909
00:46:28,160 --> 00:46:31,550
It's lighthearted, modest, and often gets a little smile.

910
00:46:32,890 --> 00:46:35,070
Okay, now we get into the really human stuff.

911
00:46:35,610 --> 00:46:38,200
The honest answers for when things are maybe a bit tough.

912
00:46:38,880 --> 00:46:40,500
Starting with hanging in there.

913
00:46:41,120 --> 00:46:42,770
Oh, this is such a relatable phrase.

914
00:46:43,070 --> 00:46:43,910
So honest.

915
00:46:44,170 --> 00:46:48,910
You use this when you're definitely not giving up, but life's throwing stuff at you.

916
00:46:48,950 --> 00:46:50,650
Yeah, you're coping, but it's work.

917
00:46:50,920 --> 00:46:51,740
Exactly.

918
00:46:51,860 --> 00:46:56,340
Hanging in there means you're managing, you're getting through a tough time, you're surviving.

919
00:46:56,380 --> 00:47:01,350
It's honest, which people appreciate, but it doesn't demand they listen to all your problems.

920
00:47:01,470 --> 00:47:03,930
It signals resilience without being a burden.

921
00:47:04,210 --> 00:47:08,560
And related to that, another one showing resilience with a strong image, Roland with the punches.

922
00:47:08,620 --> 00:47:10,060
Yeah, that's a great visual, isn't it?

923
00:47:10,260 --> 00:47:10,880
From boxing.

924
00:47:11,280 --> 00:47:14,390
The punches are life's challenges, the unexpected problems.

925
00:47:14,970 --> 00:47:15,070
Yeah.

926
00:47:15,050 --> 00:47:21,110
To roll with the punches means you're adapting, you're being flexible, you're taking the hits but not letting them knock you out.

927
00:47:21,670 --> 00:47:27,430
It's a fantastic answer if you're navigating, say, a really difficult project at work or just a stressful period.

928
00:47:27,870 --> 00:47:28,870
Shows determination.

929
00:47:29,000 --> 00:47:32,610
Now, this next one, this requires careful handling, apparently.

930
00:47:33,050 --> 00:47:33,910
Living the dream.

931
00:47:34,150 --> 00:47:34,930
Oh, absolutely.

932
00:47:34,950 --> 00:47:38,060
99% of the time, this is pure sarcasm.

933
00:47:38,320 --> 00:47:40,640
Okay, so you don't actually mean you're living the dream.

934
00:47:40,780 --> 00:47:41,420
Almost never.

935
00:47:41,460 --> 00:47:46,010
You use it when you're having a ridiculously busy, stressful, exhausting day.

936
00:47:46,070 --> 00:47:47,830
The complete opposite of a dream.

937
00:47:48,010 --> 00:47:48,190
Right.

938
00:47:48,490 --> 00:47:49,270
Give me an example.

939
00:47:49,250 --> 00:47:49,610
Okay.

940
00:47:50,160 --> 00:47:52,060
Imagine you're swamped with deadlines.

941
00:47:52,240 --> 00:47:53,320
The coffee machine's broken.

942
00:47:53,400 --> 00:47:54,640
You got stuck in traffic.

943
00:47:55,000 --> 00:47:57,810
Your colleague walks past, cheerfully asks, how are you?

944
00:47:58,410 --> 00:48:02,960
You sigh, maybe give a tired little laugh and say, oh, you know, living the dream.

945
00:48:02,980 --> 00:48:04,000
Okay.

946
00:48:04,480 --> 00:48:05,820
The tone is everything.

947
00:48:06,080 --> 00:48:06,820
Everything.

948
00:48:06,800 --> 00:48:08,190
The source stresses this.

949
00:48:08,970 --> 00:48:13,780
You need that smile or the tired laugh or maybe a bit of an eye roll to signal the irony.

950
00:48:14,340 --> 00:48:18,360
If you say it street-faced, people will genuinely think you're bragging or being weird.

951
00:48:18,680 --> 00:48:19,540
Got it.

952
00:48:19,560 --> 00:48:21,820
It's about that shared understanding of the sarcasm.

953
00:48:23,340 --> 00:48:27,220
Okay, moving to more professional updates, maybe about work or a project.

954
00:48:27,610 --> 00:48:29,150
First, the optimistic one.

955
00:48:30,090 --> 00:48:31,110
So far, so good.

956
00:48:31,410 --> 00:48:33,210
Perfect for when you're in the middle of something.

957
00:48:33,230 --> 00:48:35,030
A project, a busy week.

958
00:48:35,270 --> 00:48:38,140
It means up to this point in time, everything's going okay.

959
00:48:38,740 --> 00:48:40,100
It's positive but cautious.

960
00:48:40,440 --> 00:48:40,940
Exactly.

961
00:48:41,220 --> 00:48:46,880
It's hopeful, shows things are on track, but it wisely avoids making promises about the final outcome.

962
00:48:47,140 --> 00:48:48,950
You're not counting your chickens before they hatch.

963
00:48:49,430 --> 00:48:51,630
Shows competence without overpromising.

964
00:48:51,940 --> 00:48:54,030
Then there's the one that suggests steady effort.

965
00:48:54,750 --> 00:48:55,490
Plugging along.

966
00:48:55,750 --> 00:49:01,040
Yeah, plugging along paints a picture of consistent, perhaps slow, but steady progress.

967
00:49:01,120 --> 00:49:04,340
Like a reliable little engine just chugging away step by step.

968
00:49:04,360 --> 00:49:05,880
So it signals dedication.

969
00:49:06,070 --> 00:49:07,790
Dedication, continuity, effort.

970
00:49:08,010 --> 00:49:09,170
It's humble but positive.

971
00:49:09,210 --> 00:49:11,890
Shows you're working reliably without needing fanfare.

972
00:49:11,870 --> 00:49:18,080
OK, the next one is interesting because the source says it's actually an invitation to talk more better than yesterday.

973
00:49:18,140 --> 00:49:19,460
Yes, this one comes with a condition.

974
00:49:19,620 --> 00:49:23,810
If you say better than yesterday, you're basically putting up a sign saying, ask me what happened yesterday.

975
00:49:24,630 --> 00:49:24,870
Right.

976
00:49:25,330 --> 00:49:28,220
So you need to be prepared for that inevitable follow up question.

977
00:49:28,430 --> 00:49:30,160
Oh, what was wrong with yesterday?

978
00:49:30,920 --> 00:49:36,030
Don't use it unless you're actually willing and maybe even hoping to elaborate a bit.

979
00:49:36,210 --> 00:49:38,750
But it can be a good conversation starter if you do want to talk.

980
00:49:38,910 --> 00:49:39,370
Absolutely.

981
00:49:39,840 --> 00:49:47,550
It shows honesty, signals improvement, and can lead to a more meaningful chat with someone you trust, like a friend or maybe your manager.

982
00:49:48,130 --> 00:49:51,920
And finally, the super casual, maybe slightly younger vibe.

983
00:49:52,460 --> 00:49:53,520
All good in the hood.

984
00:49:53,640 --> 00:49:53,860
Yeah.

985
00:49:53,960 --> 00:49:57,200
Very informal, often heard in more relaxed or youthful circles.

986
00:49:57,950 --> 00:50:05,200
Hood here is just slang for neighborhood or more generally, my area, my world, my life.

987
00:50:05,470 --> 00:50:07,140
So it just means everything's fine with me.

988
00:50:07,200 --> 00:50:07,960
Yep.

989
00:50:07,980 --> 00:50:13,350
It's a very laid back, friendly way to say everything's OK. Really designed just to be warm and maybe get a little smile.

990
00:50:13,650 --> 00:50:15,210
Definitely know your audience with this one.

991
00:50:15,190 --> 00:50:18,660
Okay, that gives us a brilliant range of options for answering that key question.

992
00:50:19,140 --> 00:50:21,450
Moving on from expressing ourselves to working together.

993
00:50:21,870 --> 00:50:25,760
Part four is about the art of collaboration, agreeing and disagreeing.

994
00:50:26,040 --> 00:50:30,530
So crucial for teamwork, trust, feeling like you're on the same page.

995
00:50:30,730 --> 00:50:31,350
Absolutely.

996
00:50:31,390 --> 00:50:34,570
And agreeing effectively, it's more than just saying yes.

997
00:50:34,780 --> 00:50:39,540
It's about making the other person feel genuinely heard, understood, maybe even smart.

998
00:50:39,700 --> 00:50:39,880
Right.

999
00:50:40,260 --> 00:50:41,000
It validates them.

1000
00:50:41,320 --> 00:50:41,840
Exactly.

1001
00:50:41,880 --> 00:50:43,740
Agreement shows understanding.

1002
00:50:43,930 --> 00:50:46,810
It confirms you're on the same team, as the source puts it.

1003
00:50:46,830 --> 00:50:51,890
OK, let's look at strong agreement first, starting with that single power word, exactly.

1004
00:50:51,880 --> 00:50:52,600
Exactly.

1005
00:50:52,940 --> 00:50:53,460
So strong.

1006
00:50:53,540 --> 00:50:54,060
So clear.

1007
00:50:54,180 --> 00:50:55,220
Total confirmation.

1008
00:50:55,280 --> 00:50:57,900
It says, what you just said, 100% true.

1009
00:50:58,620 --> 00:50:59,890
I reached the same conclusion.

1010
00:51:00,430 --> 00:51:03,230
It gives the other person that immediate hit of validation.

1011
00:51:03,730 --> 00:51:04,410
Huge impact.

1012
00:51:04,810 --> 00:51:05,130
One word.

1013
00:51:05,330 --> 00:51:06,490
Similar vibe using numbers.

1014
00:51:06,510 --> 00:51:07,210
100.

1015
00:51:07,350 --> 00:51:07,650
Yeah.

1016
00:51:07,670 --> 00:51:08,450
Or 100%.

1017
00:51:08,660 --> 00:51:09,500
Very confident.

1018
00:51:09,900 --> 00:51:10,780
Often quite casual.

1019
00:51:10,840 --> 00:51:11,760
It means complete agreement.

1020
00:51:11,800 --> 00:51:12,660
No reservations.

1021
00:51:13,120 --> 00:51:13,680
Not 99%.

1022
00:51:14,300 --> 00:51:14,840
The full 100.

1023
00:51:14,960 --> 00:51:16,020
But quick and clear.

1024
00:51:16,260 --> 00:51:16,780
Very quick.

1025
00:51:16,890 --> 00:51:17,410
Very clear.

1026
00:51:17,530 --> 00:51:21,830
Great for fast consensus in, say, a team huddle or a casual chat.

1027
00:51:22,000 --> 00:51:27,100
Now for maybe more formal situations or just showing maximum respect, couldn't agree more.

1028
00:51:27,400 --> 00:51:31,030
This one sounds negative because of couldn't, but it actually means the opposite.

1029
00:51:31,160 --> 00:51:34,370
It means my level of agreement is already at the absolute maximum.

1030
00:51:34,450 --> 00:51:36,750
It's impossible for me to agree more than I already do.

1031
00:51:37,290 --> 00:51:38,210
Ah, okay.

1032
00:51:38,230 --> 00:51:40,190
So it's very strong agreement.

1033
00:51:40,210 --> 00:51:43,950
Extremely strong and very polite, very respectful.

1034
00:51:44,320 --> 00:51:49,560
A really good one to use when agreeing with maybe a senior colleague or a client.

1035
00:51:50,280 --> 00:51:51,900
Diplomatic and powerful.

1036
00:51:52,290 --> 00:51:55,660
These next two suggest you're really in sync, like you're sharing a brain.

1037
00:51:56,220 --> 00:52:00,490
That's what I was thinking, and the very visual, you took the words right out of my mouth.

1038
00:52:00,690 --> 00:52:02,830
Yeah, both excellent for showing connection.

1039
00:52:03,110 --> 00:52:04,070
That's what I was thinking.

1040
00:52:04,760 --> 00:52:07,620
Implies you had the exact same thought, maybe even at the same time.

1041
00:52:07,860 --> 00:52:10,600
Creates a really strong sense of intellectual alignment.

1042
00:52:10,880 --> 00:52:12,630
And took the words out of my mouth.

1043
00:52:12,770 --> 00:52:13,750
Even more vivid.

1044
00:52:14,450 --> 00:52:16,250
And very flattering to the other person.

1045
00:52:16,350 --> 00:52:21,160
It suggests they phrased the idea so perfectly it was exactly how you were about to say it yourself.

1046
00:52:21,840 --> 00:52:24,200
Makes them feel eloquent and deeply understood.

1047
00:52:24,330 --> 00:52:27,200
And lastly, for agreement, showing that team spirit.

1048
00:52:27,460 --> 00:52:28,160
I'm with you on that.

1049
00:52:28,460 --> 00:52:30,020
Simple, direct, supportive.

1050
00:52:30,380 --> 00:52:32,300
I'm with you means I support you.

1051
00:52:32,340 --> 00:52:33,260
I'm on your side.

1052
00:52:33,910 --> 00:52:37,630
Adding on that ties your support specifically to the point they just made.

1053
00:52:37,790 --> 00:52:38,690
Very collaborative.

1054
00:52:39,070 --> 00:52:39,970
Highly collaborative.

1055
00:52:40,250 --> 00:52:43,200
Reinforces that feeling of being a unit, working together.

1056
00:52:43,660 --> 00:52:43,940
Okay.

1057
00:52:44,540 --> 00:52:46,780
Agreement is usually the easy part.

1058
00:52:46,890 --> 00:52:50,500
Now for the trickier skill, polite disagreement.

1059
00:52:51,080 --> 00:52:53,140
This is where wires often get crossed, isn't it?

1060
00:52:53,220 --> 00:52:53,970
You're absolutely right.

1061
00:52:54,010 --> 00:52:55,930
This is definitely an advanced social skill.

1062
00:52:56,050 --> 00:53:00,210
And the goal isn't to avoid disagreeing ever that's not realistic or helpful.

1063
00:53:00,340 --> 00:53:00,510
Right.

1064
00:53:00,610 --> 00:53:01,980
You need to share different ideas.

1065
00:53:02,000 --> 00:53:02,420
Exactly.

1066
00:53:02,440 --> 00:53:09,970
The skill is sharing those alternative ideas gently in a way that doesn't shut the other person down or damage the relationship.

1067
00:53:10,530 --> 00:53:16,380
And the key, according to the source, is structure, specifically using a validation buffer.

1068
00:53:16,360 --> 00:53:17,520
A validation buffer.

1069
00:53:17,540 --> 00:53:18,160
Okay, what's that?

1070
00:53:18,640 --> 00:53:24,050
Like starting with phrases like, I see your point, but, or I get where you're coming from, however.

1071
00:53:24,110 --> 00:53:25,310
Precisely those.

1072
00:53:25,450 --> 00:53:27,720
You start by acknowledging their perspective first.

1073
00:53:27,740 --> 00:53:28,200
But hang on.

1074
00:53:28,460 --> 00:53:29,480
What if I don't see their point?

1075
00:53:29,600 --> 00:53:31,060
If I think they're completely off track?

1076
00:53:31,720 --> 00:53:36,410
Isn't saying I see your point a bit dishonest then?

1077
00:53:37,200 --> 00:53:39,020
How do you use that buffer authentically?

1078
00:53:39,080 --> 00:53:41,710
That is such a great question and a really common concern.

1079
00:53:41,990 --> 00:53:46,820
The key is the buffer isn't necessarily validating the content of their idea.

1080
00:53:46,840 --> 00:53:49,480
It's validating the person and their thought process.

1081
00:53:50,060 --> 00:53:51,440
OK, explain that more.

1082
00:53:51,720 --> 00:53:57,010
Saying I see your point or I understand where you're coming from means I've listened to you.

1083
00:53:57,330 --> 00:54:02,720
I can follow the logical steps you took to arrive at your conclusion, even if I don't agree with the conclusion itself.

1084
00:54:03,480 --> 00:54:06,220
And I respect the fact that you thought about it and shared it.

1085
00:54:06,480 --> 00:54:10,330
Okay, so you're validating their effort and logic, not the idea itself.

1086
00:54:10,450 --> 00:54:10,870
Exactly.

1087
00:54:10,950 --> 00:54:18,260
You show empathy first, then the but or however gently signals that you have a different perspective to offer.

1088
00:54:18,320 --> 00:54:22,260
It softens the whole interaction massively because you've shown respect up front.

1089
00:54:22,900 --> 00:54:23,920
That makes so much more sense.

1090
00:54:24,220 --> 00:54:24,500
Okay.

1091
00:54:24,960 --> 00:54:25,740
Reframing complete.

1092
00:54:25,820 --> 00:54:27,190
Moving on to neutral acceptance.

1093
00:54:28,040 --> 00:54:28,560
Fair enough.

1094
00:54:28,950 --> 00:54:29,200
Fair enough.

1095
00:54:29,340 --> 00:54:29,870
Very useful.

1096
00:54:30,350 --> 00:54:33,490
It means you acknowledge that their reasoning is, well, reasonable.

1097
00:54:34,140 --> 00:54:37,400
You accept the logic or maybe just the reality of the situation they presented.

1098
00:54:37,480 --> 00:54:38,380
Even if you don't love it.

1099
00:54:38,520 --> 00:54:39,390
Even if you don't love it.

1100
00:54:39,410 --> 00:54:45,840
It's a calm, mature way to sort of end that particular line of discussion without escalating.

1101
00:54:46,180 --> 00:54:49,620
You're not agreeing, but you're acknowledging their perspective has validity.

1102
00:54:50,270 --> 00:54:51,050
Very grown up.

1103
00:54:51,260 --> 00:54:53,250
What about when you're genuinely undecided?

1104
00:54:53,290 --> 00:54:56,810
Showing that internal debate, we have I'm torn and I'm on the fence.

1105
00:54:57,110 --> 00:54:59,400
Both excellent for showing honest uncertainty.

1106
00:54:59,420 --> 00:55:04,240
I'm torn means you literally feel pulled in two different directions.

1107
00:55:05,010 --> 00:55:07,470
You see the merit in two opposing arguments.

1108
00:55:07,630 --> 00:55:08,550
And on the fence.

1109
00:55:08,750 --> 00:55:10,380
That's the perfect visual, isn't it?

1110
00:55:10,800 --> 00:55:13,100
Sitting on the fence, not committed to either side.

1111
00:55:13,160 --> 00:55:15,040
It shows you haven't decided yet.

1112
00:55:15,020 --> 00:55:23,710
It's vulnerable in a good way and actually quite useful in collaborative settings because it invites others to maybe offer more information to help you decide.

1113
00:55:23,830 --> 00:55:26,340
Instead of just shutting down ideas you're unsure about.

1114
00:55:26,440 --> 00:55:26,940
Exactly.

1115
00:55:26,980 --> 00:55:28,380
It keeps the conversation open.

1116
00:55:28,680 --> 00:55:33,250
Now, for offering a different view diplomatically, I'd look at it a bit differently.

1117
00:55:33,610 --> 00:55:35,370
This is pure diplomatic gold.

1118
00:55:35,630 --> 00:55:38,090
Notice you're not saying your way of looking at it is wrong.

1119
00:55:38,250 --> 00:55:38,490
Right.

1120
00:55:38,930 --> 00:55:39,510
It's gentler.

1121
00:55:39,890 --> 00:55:40,540
Much gentler.

1122
00:55:40,980 --> 00:55:43,900
You're simply offering your alternative perspective.

1123
00:55:44,200 --> 00:55:45,540
Here's another way we could view this.

1124
00:55:46,200 --> 00:55:52,450
It shifts the dynamic away from conflict, my idea versus your idea, towards shared exploration.

1125
00:55:52,470 --> 00:55:54,110
Here are two ways to look at it.

1126
00:55:54,270 --> 00:55:54,680
Okay.

1127
00:55:54,700 --> 00:55:59,940
And then the humble correction, often needed when someone's learning or just gets a small detail wrong.

1128
00:56:00,100 --> 00:56:01,400
Not quite.

1129
00:56:01,460 --> 00:56:04,910
So much softer than a flat no or that's wrong, isn't it?

1130
00:56:04,890 --> 00:56:06,090
Yeah, much less harsh.

1131
00:56:06,350 --> 00:56:10,960
Not quite means you're close, but not 100% right.

1132
00:56:11,680 --> 00:56:19,360
It allows you to offer the correct information gently without making the other person feel foolish or publicly corrected, especially in front of others.

1133
00:56:19,960 --> 00:56:21,000
Good for team settings.

1134
00:56:21,860 --> 00:56:27,130
Then for building on someone's idea, but needing to add a crucial bit.

1135
00:56:27,430 --> 00:56:28,880
Could be, but here's the thing.

1136
00:56:28,960 --> 00:56:30,180
Nice structure again.

1137
00:56:30,200 --> 00:56:34,260
Could be starts with validation acknowledging their idea has possibility.

1138
00:56:35,120 --> 00:56:36,020
But here's the thing.

1139
00:56:36,610 --> 00:56:44,070
Signals you're about to introduce some additional information, maybe a constraint or factor they hadn't considered, that helps everyone see the bigger picture.

1140
00:56:44,110 --> 00:56:46,820
It feels collaborative, like you're refining the idea together.

1141
00:56:46,860 --> 00:56:50,200
And finally, the classic escape hatch for when you're just going in circles.

1142
00:56:50,440 --> 00:56:50,620
Yep.

1143
00:56:50,820 --> 00:56:51,820
Let's agree to disagree.

1144
00:56:52,070 --> 00:56:53,720
The mature exit strategy.

1145
00:56:54,080 --> 00:57:00,000
You use this when it's clear you both have fixed different opinions and continuing the argument is just going to cause friction and achieve nothing.

1146
00:57:00,220 --> 00:57:01,440
It stops things getting heated.

1147
00:57:01,710 --> 00:57:02,290
Precisely.

1148
00:57:02,330 --> 00:57:06,410
It's a classy, peaceful way to say, OK, we see this differently.

1149
00:57:06,470 --> 00:57:10,200
Let's stop discussing this point now and preserve our relationship.

1150
00:57:10,920 --> 00:57:15,560
It prioritizes harmony over winning the argument, a really valuable tool.

1151
00:57:15,880 --> 00:57:22,670
OK, that brings us nicely to part five, strengthening bonds, asking for help and gratitude.

1152
00:57:23,170 --> 00:57:24,710
And the source makes a really good point here.

1153
00:57:25,270 --> 00:57:26,930
Asking for help isn't weakness.

1154
00:57:27,250 --> 00:57:30,840
It's actually a sign of strength and importantly, trust in the people around you.

1155
00:57:30,820 --> 00:57:31,520
Totally agree.

1156
00:57:31,800 --> 00:57:33,420
But how we ask matters, right?

1157
00:57:33,660 --> 00:57:37,950
We need phrases that are polite, friendly, and crucially, don't sound demanding.

1158
00:57:38,470 --> 00:57:43,320
We want to respect the other person's time and make it easy for them to help or to say no if they need to.

1159
00:57:43,690 --> 00:57:45,660
OK, starting with a real classic idiom.

1160
00:57:45,820 --> 00:57:46,760
Could you give me a hand?

1161
00:57:46,960 --> 00:57:47,480
Give me a hand.

1162
00:57:47,620 --> 00:57:48,280
Super versatile.

1163
00:57:48,320 --> 00:57:51,480
It can literally mean physical help, like lifting something heavy.

1164
00:57:51,520 --> 00:57:51,980
Right.

1165
00:57:52,000 --> 00:57:57,450
But just as often, it's used for general help with a task, maybe hitting a deadline or figuring something out.

1166
00:57:57,910 --> 00:58:02,500
It's always polite, friendly, invokes that simple image of someone lending their support.

1167
00:58:02,700 --> 00:58:05,460
For maximum politeness, really showing you respect their time.

1168
00:58:05,760 --> 00:58:06,970
Would you mind helping me out?

1169
00:58:07,160 --> 00:58:08,750
Ugh, that would you mind?

1170
00:58:09,890 --> 00:58:12,070
Construction is wonderfully soft, isn't it?

1171
00:58:12,330 --> 00:58:13,430
Yeah, why is it so effective?

1172
00:58:13,690 --> 00:58:19,220
Because it directly asks, would this cause you inconvenience?

1173
00:58:19,240 --> 00:58:22,120
It frames the request around their potential burden.

1174
00:58:22,780 --> 00:58:27,770
And that gives them a really easy, low-pressure way to decline if they're genuinely swamped.

1175
00:58:27,850 --> 00:58:30,790
Which paradoxically makes them more likely to say yes.

1176
00:58:31,130 --> 00:58:31,730
Exactly.

1177
00:58:32,010 --> 00:58:35,240
Because they feel their time and autonomy have been respected up front.

1178
00:58:35,440 --> 00:58:36,720
They don't feel cornered.

1179
00:58:36,880 --> 00:58:42,630
And speaking of respecting time, the absolute essential first step before launching into your request.

1180
00:58:42,850 --> 00:58:43,410
Got a minute.

1181
00:58:43,590 --> 00:58:45,090
Oh, please, everyone, use this.

1182
00:58:45,210 --> 00:58:46,550
It's just basic consideration.

1183
00:58:46,570 --> 00:58:51,740
You check if they have a minute before you potentially take up 10 minutes of their time with your actual question.

1184
00:58:51,840 --> 00:58:52,020
Right.

1185
00:58:52,320 --> 00:58:53,080
What if they say no?

1186
00:58:53,340 --> 00:58:55,060
Then you just smile and say, no problem.

1187
00:58:55,080 --> 00:58:55,580
Catch you later.

1188
00:58:55,720 --> 00:58:57,300
Or, OK, maybe another time.

1189
00:58:57,480 --> 00:59:03,230
It shows you're thoughtful and prevents you from trapping someone who's rushing to a meeting or deep in concentration.

1190
00:59:03,730 --> 00:59:08,840
Okay, now for some clever, flattering ways to ask for help, especially when it involves their knowledge.

1191
00:59:09,400 --> 00:59:10,120
I love this one.

1192
00:59:10,420 --> 00:59:11,820
Can I borrow your brain for a sec?

1193
00:59:13,120 --> 00:59:13,800
It's great, isn't it?

1194
00:59:13,960 --> 00:59:14,740
Such a compliment.

1195
00:59:14,860 --> 00:59:15,430
Yeah.

1196
00:59:15,450 --> 00:59:19,650
It says, I value your thinking so much, I literally want to use your intelligence for a moment.

1197
00:59:20,710 --> 00:59:26,640
And adding for a sec makes it sound like a very small, quick ask, minimizing the perceived commitment.

1198
00:59:27,040 --> 00:59:28,620
People generally love hearing this.

1199
00:59:28,760 --> 00:59:29,880
Flattery gets you everywhere.

1200
00:59:30,060 --> 00:59:32,520
A slightly more formal but still respectful version.

1201
00:59:33,480 --> 00:59:34,850
Mind if I pick your expertise?

1202
00:59:34,970 --> 00:59:36,230
This one's a bit more targeted.

1203
00:59:36,650 --> 00:59:42,960
Picking someone's expertise usually means you want to ask them questions about their specific area of skill or knowledge.

1204
00:59:43,060 --> 00:59:44,500
It was good for asking a specialist.

1205
00:59:44,480 --> 00:59:45,160
Exactly.

1206
00:59:45,610 --> 00:59:49,830
It shows you recognize their particular talents and you value their expert opinion.

1207
00:59:50,450 --> 00:59:54,800
Very respectful way to ask for specific guidance, especially in a professional context.

1208
00:59:55,020 --> 00:59:59,650
For team situations, when you're genuinely stuck, being honest works well.

1209
00:59:59,670 --> 01:00:00,150
I'm stuck.

1210
01:00:00,490 --> 01:00:01,310
Any ideas?

1211
01:00:01,440 --> 01:00:03,160
Yeah, this is great for collaboration.

1212
01:00:03,220 --> 01:00:06,810
Admitting I'm stuck shows vulnerability, which builds trust.

1213
01:00:07,450 --> 01:00:11,550
And asking any ideas turns it into a joint problem-solving exercise.

1214
01:00:11,710 --> 01:00:12,910
Rather than fix this for me.

1215
01:00:13,140 --> 01:00:13,760
Precisely.

1216
01:00:13,820 --> 01:00:16,620
It invites them to brainstorm with you as a partner.

1217
01:00:17,140 --> 01:00:19,000
Much more collaborative dynamic.

1218
01:00:19,020 --> 01:00:24,870
And for anyone new joining a team or starting a new activity, could you show me the ropes?

1219
01:00:24,850 --> 01:00:26,590
The ropes, another great idiom.

1220
01:00:26,930 --> 01:00:31,650
It just means the basic procedures, the essential skills, how things work around here.

1221
01:00:31,870 --> 01:00:34,130
So it's a humble way to ask for orientation.

1222
01:00:34,450 --> 01:00:36,390
Very humble, very effective.

1223
01:00:36,970 --> 01:00:41,100
Asking someone to show you the ropes positions you as an eager learner.

1224
01:00:41,420 --> 01:00:44,660
And most people are quite happy to help someone who asks so respectfully.

1225
01:00:44,860 --> 01:00:47,870
And finally, the little phrase that keeps the social balance right.

1226
01:00:48,290 --> 01:00:49,910
Signaling you'll return the favor.

1227
01:00:50,710 --> 01:00:51,330
I'd owe you one.

1228
01:00:51,370 --> 01:00:51,610
Yes.

1229
01:00:52,250 --> 01:00:52,890
This is important.

1230
01:00:53,170 --> 01:00:54,430
It signals reciprocity.

1231
01:00:54,750 --> 01:00:59,480
You're acknowledging they're doing you a favor and you fully intend to pay it back in the future when they need help.

1232
01:00:59,640 --> 01:01:00,990
Keeps the relationship equitable.

1233
01:01:01,330 --> 01:01:01,870
Exactly.

1234
01:01:01,990 --> 01:01:07,730
It's a polite way of saying thank you in advance and it reassures them that you appreciate the help and won't just take it for granted.

1235
01:01:08,050 --> 01:01:09,000
Balances the scales.

1236
01:01:09,360 --> 01:01:10,940
Okay, let's flip to the other side of that coin.

1237
01:01:11,240 --> 01:01:13,780
Expressing thanks and replying warmly.

1238
01:01:14,360 --> 01:01:16,460
Small actions, big impact here.

1239
01:01:16,580 --> 01:01:17,220
Huge impact.

1240
01:01:17,240 --> 01:01:18,850
Gratitude is like social sunshine.

1241
01:01:19,250 --> 01:01:22,210
Makes the other person feel valued, seen, appreciated.

1242
01:01:22,690 --> 01:01:24,870
Let's start with a big enthusiastic thank you.

1243
01:01:25,050 --> 01:01:25,860
Thanks a ton.

1244
01:01:25,970 --> 01:01:28,270
A ton is a unit of heavyweight, right?

1245
01:01:28,590 --> 01:01:31,290
So thanks a ton just means thank you very much.

1246
01:01:31,590 --> 01:01:38,760
It conveys genuine enthusiasm and suggests the help you received felt significant, casual, friendly, warm.

1247
01:01:39,080 --> 01:01:44,040
And for when someone really helps you out of a jam, the traumatic compliment, you're a lifesaver.

1248
01:01:44,340 --> 01:01:51,810
Use this when someone genuinely rescues you from a difficult situation or saves you a lot of trouble or stress, even if it wasn't literally life or death.

1249
01:01:51,930 --> 01:01:52,370
Right.

1250
01:01:52,390 --> 01:01:56,520
Calling them a lifesaver is a really memorable, high-impact compliment.

1251
01:01:56,580 --> 01:01:57,680
Makes them feel like a hero.

1252
01:01:58,280 --> 01:02:00,160
Use it sincerely when it fits.

1253
01:02:00,140 --> 01:02:02,950
And to show the help had a real emotional impact on you.

1254
01:02:03,170 --> 01:02:03,810
That means a lot.

1255
01:02:03,970 --> 01:02:06,570
This one goes deeper than just thanking for the action.

1256
01:02:06,630 --> 01:02:09,400
You're communicating the effect their kindness had on you.

1257
01:02:09,500 --> 01:02:10,240
How it made you feel.

1258
01:02:10,600 --> 01:02:11,140
Exactly.

1259
01:02:11,180 --> 01:02:12,540
That means a lot.

1260
01:02:12,920 --> 01:02:14,490
Says their help wasn't just practical.

1261
01:02:14,670 --> 01:02:18,410
It genuinely touched your heart, made a difference to your emotional state.

1262
01:02:18,860 --> 01:02:20,060
Deeply meaningful.

1263
01:02:20,040 --> 01:02:21,780
Okay, now, replying to thanks.

1264
01:02:22,380 --> 01:02:24,780
We need to get beyond just, you're welcome, right?

1265
01:02:25,220 --> 01:02:29,450
For a really relaxed, casual vibe, the source suggests, no worries.

1266
01:02:29,690 --> 01:02:30,250
No worries.

1267
01:02:31,050 --> 01:02:35,960
Super chill, incredibly common now, especially in places like Australia, but really global.

1268
01:02:36,520 --> 01:02:39,230
It just means, honestly, it was zero trouble for me.

1269
01:02:39,250 --> 01:02:40,210
Don't stress about it.

1270
01:02:40,350 --> 01:02:41,150
Very easygoing.

1271
01:02:41,270 --> 01:02:46,620
Very easygoing, very friendly, minimizes any feeling that you went to huge effort.

1272
01:02:46,600 --> 01:02:50,780
For a more generous reply, one that explicitly invites future requests.

1273
01:02:51,320 --> 01:02:51,790
Anytime.

1274
01:02:51,990 --> 01:02:57,410
When you say anytime, you're signaling I was happy to help and please feel free to ask me again in the future.

1275
01:02:57,510 --> 01:02:59,220
So it makes them feel comfortable asking again.

1276
01:02:59,260 --> 01:02:59,760
Totally.

1277
01:02:59,800 --> 01:03:00,780
Very supportive.

1278
01:03:00,900 --> 01:03:01,780
Very comfortable.

1279
01:03:02,040 --> 01:03:04,080
Builds that feeling of reliable backup.

1280
01:03:04,150 --> 01:03:07,470
For a humble, gracious feel, we have don't mention it.

1281
01:03:07,650 --> 01:03:08,790
A real classic.

1282
01:03:09,170 --> 01:03:10,930
This one minimizes your effort.

1283
01:03:11,380 --> 01:03:16,580
The implication is, what I did was such a small thing, it's not even worth mentioning or thanking me for.

1284
01:03:16,640 --> 01:03:18,760
Makes the asker feel better about having asked.

1285
01:03:18,780 --> 01:03:19,320
Exactly.

1286
01:03:19,360 --> 01:03:20,820
Removes any sense of burden.

1287
01:03:21,180 --> 01:03:21,910
Very gracious.

1288
01:03:22,070 --> 01:03:23,890
And the positive, upbeat reply.

1289
01:03:23,930 --> 01:03:24,870
Happy to help.

1290
01:03:25,170 --> 01:03:28,570
This clearly communicates that the act of helping was actually a pleasure for you.

1291
01:03:28,630 --> 01:03:29,470
It wasn't a chore.

1292
01:03:29,490 --> 01:03:30,210
It wasn't a burden.

1293
01:03:30,370 --> 01:03:32,530
So it makes the whole interaction feel positive.

1294
01:03:32,650 --> 01:03:33,410
Absolutely.

1295
01:03:33,450 --> 01:03:40,540
By showing you enjoyed helping, you make sure the person asking feels good about the whole exchange, completes that cycle of positivity.

1296
01:03:40,860 --> 01:03:41,040
Right.

1297
01:03:41,060 --> 01:03:42,690
We are nearing the end of the interaction.

1298
01:03:42,770 --> 01:03:46,890
We've connected, chatted, maybe collaborated, shown gratitude.

1299
01:03:46,950 --> 01:03:49,240
Now we need to wrap it up smoothly.

1300
01:03:50,560 --> 01:03:53,000
Part six, the gentle exit.

1301
01:03:53,900 --> 01:03:55,910
How to say goodbye without awkwardness.

1302
01:03:56,160 --> 01:03:58,240
Yes, the awkward goodbye shuffle.

1303
01:03:58,320 --> 01:03:59,110
We've all been there.

1304
01:04:00,050 --> 01:04:08,070
These phrases are designed to signal your intention to leave clearly but politely in showing a positive final impression and keeping the door open for next time.

1305
01:04:08,390 --> 01:04:12,200
OK, first up, signaling you need to leave, but making it about your needs.

1306
01:04:12,720 --> 01:04:13,600
I'd better get going.

1307
01:04:13,680 --> 01:04:15,180
The I'd better part is key.

1308
01:04:15,420 --> 01:04:20,760
It frames leaving as a sensible decision for you, maybe because of your schedule or something you need to do.

1309
01:04:20,800 --> 01:04:22,030
Not because you're bored of them.

1310
01:04:22,250 --> 01:04:22,850
Exactly.

1311
01:04:22,930 --> 01:04:25,330
And get going is just a softer way to say leave.

1312
01:04:25,350 --> 01:04:27,490
It's polite because the focus is internal.

1313
01:04:27,610 --> 01:04:28,970
I need to rather than external.

1314
01:04:29,090 --> 01:04:29,790
I need to leave you.

1315
01:04:30,590 --> 01:04:31,640
Smooths the transition.

1316
01:04:31,700 --> 01:04:32,960
No ego involved.

1317
01:04:33,240 --> 01:04:35,840
Then showing respect for their time and the potential busyness.

1318
01:04:36,080 --> 01:04:36,980
I'll let you get back to it.

1319
01:04:37,110 --> 01:04:39,900
This is maybe the most considerate exit strategy.

1320
01:04:39,940 --> 01:04:43,220
You're explicitly acknowledging they probably have things to do.

1321
01:04:43,280 --> 01:04:43,860
Yeah.

1322
01:04:43,880 --> 01:04:46,490
And you're kindly releasing them back to their work or their day.

1323
01:04:46,570 --> 01:04:51,330
It makes you sound really thoughtful, very aware of their time, polite and respectful.

1324
01:04:51,730 --> 01:04:51,830
Okay.

1325
01:04:51,850 --> 01:04:55,000
Now setting up the next interaction, keeping the connection alive.

1326
01:04:55,660 --> 01:04:56,640
Let's catch up soon.

1327
01:04:56,700 --> 01:05:01,380
To catch up means, you know, talk again in the near future, share news, see what's been happening.

1328
01:05:01,450 --> 01:05:04,640
Using let's catch up soon leaves that door wide open.

1329
01:05:04,980 --> 01:05:06,890
Expresses a desire to reconnect.

1330
01:05:07,070 --> 01:05:08,610
A genuine desire, yeah.

1331
01:05:08,630 --> 01:05:11,880
Makes the goodbye feel temporary, warm, and forward-looking.

1332
01:05:12,040 --> 01:05:12,720
I enjoyed this.

1333
01:05:12,820 --> 01:05:13,700
Let's do it again.

1334
01:05:13,680 --> 01:05:18,670
For something maybe quicker, more standard, especially in emails or texts, talk soon.

1335
01:05:18,990 --> 01:05:20,610
Short, sweet, simple, talk soon.

1336
01:05:21,290 --> 01:05:26,490
Works perfectly face-to-face too, but it's incredibly useful at the end of calls, emails, texts.

1337
01:05:27,010 --> 01:05:30,340
Just a general positive signal of intended future contact.

1338
01:05:30,600 --> 01:05:34,520
And the absolute classic casual, relaxed goodbye.

1339
01:05:35,180 --> 01:05:35,700
Catch you later.

1340
01:05:35,840 --> 01:05:36,500
Yeah, catch you later.

1341
01:05:36,560 --> 01:05:37,780
Very common, very laid back.

1342
01:05:37,800 --> 01:05:40,330
It's like, see you later, but maybe even less formal.

1343
01:05:40,650 --> 01:05:42,710
Doesn't commit to a specific time or place.

1344
01:05:42,960 --> 01:05:44,300
It's like a friendly farewell.

1345
01:05:44,640 --> 01:05:45,160
Exactly.

1346
01:05:45,220 --> 01:05:49,550
Perfect for colleagues you see often, acquaintances, friends, easy breezy.

1347
01:05:51,650 --> 01:05:52,230
Wow.

1348
01:05:52,590 --> 01:05:53,050
Okay.

1349
01:05:53,070 --> 01:05:53,730
We've actually done it.

1350
01:05:53,790 --> 01:06:04,440
We've gone through all 75 phrases, covered all eight sections from the source material, from that very first gentle, hey there, all the way to the smooth talk soon or catch you later exit.

1351
01:06:04,740 --> 01:06:14,030
You, the listener, you now genuinely have this complete practical toolkit, not just for grammar, but for communicating with your whole heart with real social warmth.

1352
01:06:14,110 --> 01:06:16,450
It really is a fantastic toolkit for building connection.

1353
01:06:16,910 --> 01:06:19,860
But let's just emphasize that key takeaway again for you, the learner.

1354
01:06:20,340 --> 01:06:21,200
This is a deep dive.

1355
01:06:21,260 --> 01:06:24,840
Yes, a lot of phrases, but the aim is an overnight mastery.

1356
01:06:25,060 --> 01:06:25,800
Absolutely not.

1357
01:06:26,090 --> 01:06:27,190
Remember that lovely analogy.

1358
01:06:27,370 --> 01:06:30,050
Learning is like a flower opening one petal at a time.

1359
01:06:30,290 --> 01:06:30,570
Nice.

1360
01:06:30,850 --> 01:06:33,210
Don't let this big list feel overwhelming or stressful.

1361
01:06:33,230 --> 01:06:38,920
The real goal, just choose maybe one, maybe two phrases, the ones that felt good in your heart as you listen today.

1362
01:06:39,000 --> 01:06:39,980
The ones that resonated.

1363
01:06:39,960 --> 01:06:40,720
Exactly.

1364
01:06:40,820 --> 01:06:44,990
Understand the context, the feeling behind them that we discussed, and just try them out this week.

1365
01:06:45,010 --> 01:06:45,550
See how they feel.

1366
01:06:46,070 --> 01:06:54,490
Because every single time you choose one of these authentic connecting phrases instead of maybe a more stilted textbook one, that's a huge win, that success.

1367
01:06:55,050 --> 01:07:05,580
So thinking bigger picture then, this source material, it frames all these small, everyday, expressive phrases as the real key to sounding natural in English.

1368
01:07:05,760 --> 01:07:09,350
Which leads us to that final, maybe provocative thought for you to ponder.

1369
01:07:10,050 --> 01:07:17,980
If these tiny, often ignored phrases, things like no worries or saying living the dream with the right ironic tone or admitting I'm on the fence.

1370
01:07:18,800 --> 01:07:31,820
If these are the true markers of natural communication, of social connection, of warmth, then what crucial social and cultural context, what layer of human interaction does a standard dictionary or grammar book just completely miss?

1371
01:07:31,800 --> 01:07:32,100
Wow.

1372
01:07:32,560 --> 01:07:33,320
That's a big question.

1373
01:07:33,510 --> 01:07:37,130
I mean, I think the answer has to be that shared emotional shorthand, doesn't it?

1374
01:07:37,310 --> 01:07:37,830
Tell me more.

1375
01:07:38,070 --> 01:07:41,880
These phrases, they're not really about the literal dictionary definitions.

1376
01:07:41,900 --> 01:07:47,110
They're about triggering an immediate shared understanding of a feeling or a social situation.

1377
01:07:47,890 --> 01:07:50,090
Mastering them isn't about vocabulary size.

1378
01:07:50,370 --> 01:07:59,130
It's about mastering how to share your actual human state, whether you're feeling grateful or hanging in there or just genuinely happy to help.

1379
01:07:59,110 --> 01:08:10,240
That emotional honesty, that fluency in expressing and understanding the subtle social cues embedded in these simple words, that's maybe the highest level of communication mastery there is.

1380
01:08:10,550 --> 01:08:23,010
Using that shared shorthand instead of just relying on perfect sentence structure, keep practicing with those keys, those little phrases, keep listening for the music, and you'll find that door to natural English connection really does start to open wide.

1381
01:08:23,390 --> 01:08:24,210
Fantastic advice.

1382
01:08:24,650 --> 01:08:26,430
Until next time, keep diving deep.

1383
01:08:35,960 --> 01:08:39,000
Bye.
